  "slug": "22q11-deletion-syndrome-c722",
  "summary": "Recent preprints tie oligodendroglial defects to interneuron hypomyelination and altered network organization in neurodevelopmental models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is oligodendrocyte lineage and myelination as a point of vulnerability in neurodevelopmental disorder models. In cntnap2-/- autism model mice, transient OPC differentiation failure produces hypomyelination of parvalbumin interneurons, with circuit dysfunction and social deficits. In a 22q11.2 deletion mouse model, mutant prefrontal cortex at P28 shows increased immature and mature oligodendrocytes alongside reduced myelination. Separately, personalized functional brain networks in 22q11.2 deletion syndrome show reorganization beyond global size reduction, indicating that network scaling differs from anatomy. EPHB2 variants, from a distinct axon-guidance context, cause callosal dysgenesis through cell-autonomous long-range axon outgrowth. What is new is the explicit linkage of glial maturation timing to interneuron myelination and behavior.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
